<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Satsuma Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Seinan War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> Rebellion was a rising of former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a> against the Meiji government in 1877, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigo Takamori</a>. Angered by the loss of their privileges and stipends, the rebels besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto">Kumamoto</a> Castle but were defeated by the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> army. Saigo died at the Battle of Shiroyama in September 1877, and the defeat ended armed samurai resistance to the modernising state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/satsuma-rebellion.jpg" alt="Satsuma Rebellion" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hoodinski · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satsuma_rebeliion.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> had been one of the key players in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a>, and many of its men had risen to influential posts, yet dissatisfaction grew. Modernisation abolished the privileged status of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> and undermined their finances. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a>, a senior leader of the Satsuma clan, was concerned about political corruption and had strongly supported war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in the Seikanron debate of 1873. When the plan was rejected he resigned all his government positions and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a>, as did many other Satsuma ex-samurai. In 1874 he founded a private academy in Kagoshima, the Shi-gakkō, with 132 branches in the prefecture. By the end of 1876 Satsuma had effectively seceded from the central government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In December 1876 the government sent a police officer, Nakahara Hisao, and 57 other men to investigate unrest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>. They were captured and, under torture, confessed to having been sent to assassinate Saigō; Nakahara later repudiated the confession, but it was widely believed.</p><p>On 30 January 1877 the government sent a warship to remove weapons from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a> arsenal, and students from Saigō's academy raided arsenals in reply. Saigō was reluctantly persuaded to lead the rebellion, and on 12 February he announced that he would march to Tokyo to ask questions of the government. His army besieged Kumamoto Castle. The main Imperial Army reached Kumamoto on 12 April and put the besiegers to flight. The eight-day Battle of Tabaruzaka left each side with more than 4,000 killed or wounded.</p><p>Saigō retreated, and by 17 August his army had been reduced to 3,000 combatants. With about 500 men he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a> on 1 September and seized Shiroyama, where General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamagata-aritomo">Yamagata Aritomo</a>'s troops, which outnumbered the rebels 60 to 1, built ditches and walls around them. The final assault came on 24 September 1877. Saigō was severely wounded, and by the most widely accepted accounts he committed seppuku with his associate Beppu Shinsuke as his second. An autopsy found no abdominal wounds consistent with seppuku, which suggests he was beheaded to prevent capture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Crushing the rebellion cost the government ¥420,000,000, forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> off the gold standard and led it to print paper currency. The rebellion effectively ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> class, because the new army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a> had proved itself in battle. The historian Mark Ravina argues that the name War of the Southwest suits the conflict better than Satsuma Rebellion, as it was closer to a civil war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Satsuma Rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Discontent led to the Saga Rebellion and the <b>Satsuma Rebellion</b> led by Saigō Takamori, put down by the new Imperial Japanese Army; yet most samurai found new work as officials, teachers and officers.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The Battōtai (&quot;Drawn-Sword Unit&quot;) was a squad of police swordsmen formed by the Meiji government in 1877, during the <b>Satsuma Rebellion</b>, the uprising led by Saigō Takamori.</blockquote><a href="/songs/battotai">Battōtai</a></li><li><blockquote>The battle ended the <b>Satsuma Rebellion</b>, the last internal mutiny in the Empire of Japan.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-24">24 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It became Kagoshima Prefecture in 1871; Saigo's <b>Satsuma Rebellion</b> of 1877 was crushed.</blockquote><a href="/state/satsuma-domain">Satsuma Domain</a></li></ul></section>
